trategy, and my leadership team is in place. We are officially in the Planning Phase.\\n\\nMy immediate priority is to delegate the initial planning tasks to Director Tarkin (Engineering) and Director Lagret (Product & Design). They will be responsible for creating the foundational components of our Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP).\\n\\nDirector Tarkin will focus on the technical infrastructure, outlining the steps for setting up the website's environment. Director Lagret will focus on the site's architecture and content strategy, ensuring it aligns with the user's requirements for simplicity and clarity.\\n\\nI will now issue two `ORDER` system calls to my directors, providing them with clear instructions to begin their work. Once they report back with their respective plans, I will integrate them into a unified DEP for final review.\\n\\n###order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Chief Delta\\nTo: System\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director Tarkin, welcome. The user has approved our proposal to build an HOA website using a CMS like WordPress. Your first task is to create the technical portion of our Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This plan must contain a clear, step-by-step list of directives for a non-technical user to execute. The plan should cover: 1. How to choose and purchase a suitable domain name. 2. How to select and purchase a web hosting plan that supports WordPress. 3. The process for installing WordPress. 4. A list of recommended essential plugins for security (e.g., Wordfence), backups (e.g., UpdraftPlus), and a contact form (e.g., WPForms), along with instructions for their installation. Assume the user has zero technical expertise. Report back with this detailed list of directives.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"5bec55c9-8d15-4cac-bcdb-8210d3195232\\\"\\n}\\n###order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z###\\n\\n###order-lagret-sitemap-plan-20240520T120001Z###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Chief Delta\\nTo: System\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director Lagret, welcome. The user has approved building a user-friendly HOA website. Your first task is to create the product and design portion of our Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). For this initial phase, focus on the website's information architecture. Please produce: 1. A comprehensive sitemap that outlines all pages for both the public-facing sections (e.g., Home, News/Updates, Contact) and the private, resident-only sections (e.g., Resident Login, Meeting Minutes, Financial Reports, Community Rules). 2. A high-level content outline for each page, describing the type of information the user will need to gather or write. This will serve as a content-gathering guide for the user. Report back with the sitemap and content outline.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"a78ed4f1-6de1-46d7-af53-606b3f581b3f\\\"\\n}\\n###order-lagret-sitemap-plan-20240520T120001Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###REPORT-order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Tarkin (5bec55c9-8d15-4cac-bcdb-8210d3195232)\\nTo: e5aabc87-5581-4e1f-9a85-b5a3d94ad2ad\\nType: Reporting\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Director Tarkin reporting. The technical portion of the Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) is complete. It has been compiled from a report by Supervisor Drayven and my own analysis, ensuring it is suitable for a non-technical user.\\\\n\\\\nHere is the complete DEP:\\\\n\\\\n**Deterministically Executable Plan: HOA Website Setup**\\\\n\\\\n**Part 1: How to Choose and Buy Your Website's Address (Domain Name)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 1.1: Understand Your Website's Address.** A domain name is the unique address for your website on the internet, like `YourHOAName.com`. This is what people will type into a web browser to find you.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.2: Brainstorm Names.** Choose a name that is simple and directly related to your HOA. Good examples are `MapleCreekHOA.com` or `SunnyvaleHomes.org`. Write down a few options in case your first choice is taken.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.3: Pick an Ending (.com or .org).** The part after the name (like `.com`) is the extension. For an HOA, `.com` is the most common and recommended choice. `.org` is also a good option, as it is often used for organizations.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.4: Visit a Domain Seller's Website.** We will use a company called a 'domain registrar' to buy the name. Please open your web browser and go to `www.namecheap.com`.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.5: Search for Your Name.** On the Namecheap homepage, type your first-choice domain name into the main search box and click the 'Search' button.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.6: Check if Your Name is Available.** The website will tell you if the name can be purchased. If it's taken, try another name from your brainstorm list until you find one that is available.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.7: Add the Domain to Your Cart.** Once you find an available name you like, click the 'Add to Cart' button next to it.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.8: Proceed to Checkout and Decline Extras.** During checkout, you may be offered extras like 'Web Hosting' or 'Private Email'. You do not need any of these, so decline them. Ensure that 'Domain Privacy' is enabled, as it is usually free and protects your personal information. Then, proceed to finalize your order.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.9: Create an Account and Pay.** Follow the on-screen instructions to create an account with Namecheap and pay for your domain name. Be sure to save your username and password in a safe place.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 2: How to Rent Space for Your Website (Web Hosting)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 2.1: Understand Web Hosting.** Web hosting is a service that stores your website's files and makes them available for people to see online. It's like renting a piece of land on the internet for your website to live on.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.2: Visit a Recommended Host.** We need a host that works well with WordPress (the software we'll use to build the site). Please open your web browser and go to `www.bluehost.com`.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.3: Choose a Hosting Plan.** On the Bluehost website, navigate to 'Hosting' and select 'Shared Hosting'. This is the most popular and affordable option for new websites.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.4: Select the 'Basic' Plan.** You will see several plans. The 'Basic' plan provides everything you need to get started. Click the 'Select' button for the 'Basic' plan.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.5: Connect Your Domain Name.** The next screen will ask for a domain name. Since you already bought one, choose the option 'Use a domain you own'. Type the domain name you purchased from Namecheap into the box and click 'Next'.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.6: Create Your Hosting Account.** Fill in the required account and contact information to create your Bluehost account.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.7: Choose Your Plan Length and Uncheck Extras.** In the 'Package Information' section, you can choose how long to pay for hosting (12 months is a good starting point). Below this, in 'Package Extras', uncheck all the boxes for optional add-ons like 'Codeguard Basic' or 'SiteLock Security'. These are not necessary right now.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.8: Complete the Purchase.** Enter your payment information and agree to the terms of service to complete your purchase. Save your Bluehost username and password in a safe place. You now own your domain name and the hosting space for your website.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 3: Installing the Website Building Software (WordPress)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 3.1: Log into your Bluehost Account.** Go to `www.bluehost.com` and log in using the credentials you just created.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.2: Start the Website Creation Process.** Bluehost will likely guide you with a setup wizard. If not, look for a section called 'My Sites' in the dashboard on the left and click the 'Create Site' or 'Add Site' button.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.3: Enter Your Site's Name and Tagline.** When prompted, enter a name for your site (e.g., 'Maple Creek HOA') and an optional tagline (e.g., 'Your community resource').\\\\n*   **Directive 3.4: Select Your Domain.** The installer will ask which domain you want to install WordPress on. Select the domain name you purchased from Namecheap from the dropdown list.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.5: Complete the Installation.** Bluehost will now automatically install WordPress on your domain. This may take a few minutes.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.6: Note Your WordPress Login Details.** Once installation is complete, Bluehost will show you your WordPress login URL (e.g., `YourHOAName.com/wp-admin`), username, and password. **This is critically important.** Save these details in a secure location. They are different from your Bluehost account login.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.7: Access Your Website's Control Panel.** Open a new browser tab and go to your WordPress login URL (`YourHOAName.com/wp-admin`). Log in using the WordPress username and password you just saved. You are now in the control panel for your website.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 4: Installing Essential Tools (Plugins)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 4.1: Understand Plugins.** Plugins are like apps for your website. They add new features, like security protection or contact forms. We will install three essential plugins from your WordPress control panel (also called the dashboard).\\\\n*   **Directive 4.2: Go to the 'Add Plugins' Screen.** In your WordPress dashboard, find the 'Plugins' menu item on the left-hand side and click 'Add New'.\\\\n*   **Security Plugin (Wordfence)**\\\\n*   **Directive 4.3: Search for Wordfence.** In the search bar on the top right, type `Wordfence Security`.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.4: Install Wordfence.** You will see a box for 'Wordfence Security – Firewall & Malware Scan'. Click the 'Install Now' button. Wait for it to finish.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.5: Activate Wordfence.** After it installs, the button will change to 'Activate'. Click it.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.6: Complete Wordfence Setup.** Wordfence will launch a setup wizard. Enter an email address where you want to receive security alerts. Agree to the terms and accept the default settings to complete the setup.\\\\n*   **Backup Plugin (UpdraftPlus)**\\\\n*   **Directive 4.7: Search for UpdraftPlus.** Return to the 'Plugins' > 'Add New' screen. In the search bar, type `UpdraftPlus`.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.8: Install UpdraftPlus.** Find the 'UpdraftPlus WordPress Backup Plugin' and click 'Install Now'.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.9: Activate UpdraftPlus.** After it installs, click the 'Activate' button.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.10: Configure UpdraftPlus.** After activation, you will be prompted to configure it. Follow the on-screen instructions. Go to the 'Settings' tab and connect a remote storage option like Google Drive, Dropbox, or another service you use. This ensures your backups are stored safely off-site.\\\\n*   **Contact Form Plugin (WPForms)**\\\\n*   **Directive 4.11: Search for WPForms.** Return to the 'Plugins' > 'Add New' screen and search for `WPForms`.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.12: Install WPForms.** Find 'Contact Form by WPForms – Drag & Drop Form Builder for WordPress'. Click 'Install Now'.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.13: Activate WPForms.** After it installs, click the 'Activate' button. The free 'Lite' version will be installed, which is sufficient for our initial needs.\\\\n\\\\nThis concludes the initial setup plan. I await further orders.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderID\\\": \\\"order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z\\\"\\n}\\n###REPORT-order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Director Tarkin has delivered a comprehensive and well-structured technical plan. This is an excellent foundation for our DEP. The directives are clear, precise, and perfectly tailored for a non-technical user, aligning with my instructions.\\n\\nI